# WHAT A SET OF DOCUMENTS DOES NOT ANSWER, BROKEN DOWN BY WHY. One row per (question, reason),
# with how many times that pairing occurs across whichever documents the caller chose.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THE SCOPE IS A SLOT, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE FILE. "Some relation, over some set
#    of documents" is the shape of every finding here, and choosing the document set is the
#    single decision most easily forgotten -- forget it and a report about the world quietly
#    counts the stipulations written to exercise it. A slot cannot be forgotten: a caller that
#    supplies no `@scope` does not compose at all. The choice moves from a habit into the
#    build.
#
# ⭐⭐ AND THE TWO ANSWERS ARE DIFFERENT QUESTIONS, WHICH IS WHY BOTH CALLERS EXIST.
#    Over the corpus, this reads as WHAT NOBODY HAS MEASURED YET -- the honest map of the
#    evidence's edges, and where the next instrument is worth building.
#    Over the fixtures, it reads as WHAT THIS REPOSITORY HAD TO INVENT -- every absence a
#    stipulation had to assert because no real filing produced it, which is the list of states
#    the model can handle and has never actually met.
-- epistemics/absences.sqlc, restricted by the caller's @scope.
SELECT a.question, a.reason, count(*) AS times
FROM (
    :compose(epistemics/absences.sqlc)
) a
JOIN (
    :compose(@scope)
) s USING (filing)
GROUP BY 1, 2
ORDER BY 3 DESC, 1, 2
